 "risk": "medium",
 "finding": "`~/.config/opencode/opencode.json` is 213 bytes and declares NO `agent` key - `jq -r 'has(\"agent\")'` on it prints false. It sets only `default_agent: \"build\"` and a permission block whose `edit` and `bash` are both `allow`. The redblue checkout has no opencode.json (`ls redblue/opencode.json` -> No such file or directory). rbops/dispatch.sh:786 invokes opencode as `--dir \"$PROJECT_DIR\" --agent reviewer`; opencode resolves configuration from the working directory upward and then the global file, so the name `reviewer` is not found there and opencode falls back to `default_agent: build` - a configuration that grants edit and bash. The reviewer is contractually read-only: rbops/agents/reviewer.md opens 'You are READ-ONLY: you cannot edit files or run commands', and the pipeline's own opencode.json sets `agent.reviewer.permission` to `edit: deny, bash: deny`. NEITHER is in the resolution path. dispatch.sh:727-732 already acknowledges the resolution failure in prose and inlines the CONTRACT, which is why every phase has had one - but the PERMISSIONS remain unenforced, so the single component whose job is to catch a compromised phase is the one component that is allowed to edit it, and nothing in the gate would notice if it did.",
